# `data[w]` appears in the loop header and in the loop body. The holder and the key are parameters that the compiler knows nothing about.
# Their use alone suggests "an array of arrays and an index". When the arguments were neither, the generic version ran: 10,710 instructions for a hundred iterations, where plain Bun takes 885.
# Now the row is looked up in the preheader, as a row of a proven array is.
# The lookup is the operation that runs no code and returns absent for everything except an own data property of a plain object under a string key.
# If the row is found, the code dispatches on what the row is (an array of numbers, an array, bytes, typed arrays) and remembers the row across the iterations.
# If the operation returns absent, the loop runs as it was built before. A hundred iterations now take 847 instructions.
# Here the key names an accessor, so twelve iterations call the getter twenty-five times.
# Fault injection, testing=UseGenericOwnKeyReads: the lookup in the preheader takes the generic path, which runs code. The getter is called once, silently.
